WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:18.880
I will be very brief, Chair.

00:00:18.880 --> 00:00:27.859
I wanted to highlight first of all the scale
of ambition set out in this paper.

00:00:27.859 --> 00:00:36.430
We are promising to develop new capitated
funding models, a new MCP contract, a PACS

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:43.780
contract, a blended pay-for-performance mechanism
that combines CQUIN and QOF, support through

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:53.670
public procurement, new organisational forms,
support through information governance arrangements,

00:00:53.670 --> 00:01:02.411
a comprehensive approach to evaluation and
monitoring, logic models, dashboards, new

00:01:02.411 --> 00:01:12.299
workforce roles, support on workforce strategy
locally, as well as public and patient engagement

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:18.490
and the implementation of new technology.

00:01:18.490 --> 00:01:27.020
So the programme is comprehensive, it is holistic
and it is ambitious.

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:34.719
Secondly, the way in which the support programme
has been developed and will be developed is

00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:44.759
based on four principles of programme design,
which reflects our approach to change management.

00:01:44.759 --> 00:01:52.350
First of all, the principle that we are working
jointly with local sites to solve problems

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.520
through joint national local leadership.

00:01:54.520 --> 00:02:00.210
Each of the workstreams set out in the support
package will have a national leader and local

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:04.759
leaders working together to try and solve
problems.

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:15.020
Secondly, the aim of those workstreams is
to create simple, where possible standard

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:22.330
recipes; replicable frameworks which local
systems can then decide to adapt and tailor

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.660
if they wish.

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:33.120
That approach is absolutely vital to enabling
subsequent easy spread of good practice.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:40.550
Thirdly, we are looking to encourage and support
radical innovation.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:48.250
This programme is not about marginal incremental
change, but encouraging local systems to be

00:02:48.250 --> 00:02:51.950
bold in their thinking and their execution.

00:02:51.950 --> 00:03:00.910
Then fourthly, we clearly need to work at
pace, at the pace of the fastest vanguards,

00:03:00.910 --> 00:03:09.950
whilst at the same time looking to ensure
we understand the stages of implementation

00:03:09.950 --> 00:03:17.490
and of impact, and that the changes that are
being delivered are real.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:23.870
So that is on the change model principles.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:32.210
The paper outlined today is a summary of a
longer paper which we will publish by the

00:03:32.210 --> 00:03:35.230
end of July in the NHS England Business Plan.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:38.180
We promised we would do so by that date.

00:03:38.180 --> 00:03:45.780
We have been developing it with the vanguards
themselves, with our national partners through

00:03:45.780 --> 00:03:52.200
engagement of the National New Care Models
Board, which I chair with Adrian Masters at

00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:53.250
Monitor.

00:03:53.250 --> 00:03:59.000
It also follows on from a discussion of the
arm’s-length body chief executives earlier

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:04.040
this week, where there was strong support
and endorsement for what we are doing.

00:04:04.040 --> 00:04:11.260
We are fine-tuning and refining that document
as we speak now.

00:04:11.260 --> 00:04:17.740
My final point, Chair, is that having set
out a work programme, clearly the task for

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:24.560
us now is to crack on with the execution and
delivery of that programme.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:31.749
The publication of the support package will
enable vanguards, the wider NHS and indeed,

00:04:31.749 --> 00:04:39.389
this Board, to hold me and the New Care Models
team to account for the progress that we make.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:40.550
Thank you very much, Ian.

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:45.901
Of course, it is also the vanguards who hold
us to account for the progress that we make,

00:04:45.901 --> 00:04:52.650
and I think this is where this is such an
innovative programme; from the perspective

00:04:52.650 --> 00:04:57.900
of the Board, probably one proxy measure of
its success is the degree of discomfort that

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.380
it causes us.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:05.169
Because it is an inversion of what has sometimes
been seen as the natural order, which is that

00:05:05.169 --> 00:05:11.199
instructions are issued from this part of
the world and obeyed or not observed elsewhere.

00:05:11.199 --> 00:05:18.300
So I think that comes to a question, which
is: how confident are you that we can be as

00:05:18.300 --> 00:05:23.789
fleet of foot as the fastest vanguard, which
is the test you have just set us?

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:31.949
Do you feel the track record so far, and the
capability to be able to work around the capitation

00:05:31.949 --> 00:05:38.810
models and the other aspects of the support
package, is at a speed that will empower and

00:05:38.810 --> 00:05:43.180
not delay what the vanguards are trying to
do?

00:05:43.180 --> 00:05:49.330
I am confident, Chair, and we need to remember
that the programme only started in March.

00:05:49.330 --> 00:05:55.639
We only selected our 29 sites following the
process back then.

00:05:55.639 --> 00:05:59.509
We have completed our 29 two-day site visits.

00:05:59.509 --> 00:06:05.419
We have been engaging through Sam Jones’s
team intensively with the different vanguard

00:06:05.419 --> 00:06:11.490
systems to understand what their requirements,
and this document is our attempt to channel

00:06:11.490 --> 00:06:13.009
what their wishes are.

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:19.180
I believe that we are working as fast as we
can in terms of articulating that, and it

00:06:19.180 --> 00:06:23.960
provides a sound platform for us to progress.

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:29.479
We have considered carefully with our partners
the deliverability of the different elements

00:06:29.479 --> 00:06:34.969
within this package, and we would not be commending
it today if we did not think it is deliverable.

00:06:34.969 --> 00:06:40.400
Nonetheless, it will be challenging and stretching
for us to, as you say, proceed at the pace

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:42.900
of the fastest sites.

00:06:42.900 --> 00:06:50.639
I think we have no choice but to make sure
we step up to the plate.

00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:57.789
Ian, we have been talking for the last year
about how the NHS needs to become a world-class

00:06:57.789 --> 00:07:05.279
knowledge organisation, and embedded in the
themes of vanguards is the whole concept of

00:07:05.279 --> 00:07:11.111
spreading know-how, spreading best practice,
diffusing it and embedding it.

00:07:11.111 --> 00:07:18.669
It is important to the execution and the replication
of the vanguards, but it is also, as a competence

00:07:18.669 --> 00:07:24.719
of the NHS, important because it moves us
towards a much more agile form of delivering

00:07:24.719 --> 00:07:26.349
things.

00:07:26.349 --> 00:07:37.520
What in the support package do you have to
create a durable knowledge-sharing-type organisation,

00:07:37.520 --> 00:07:42.960
and how are they going to embed the knowledge
that is shared quickly into their roll-out

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:43.960
plans?

00:07:43.960 --> 00:07:46.599
Thank you, Noel.

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:51.150
That is a fantastic question.

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:58.830
The first point I would make is that I think
the NHS has not been great at plagiarising

00:07:58.830 --> 00:08:02.590
what is known to work well.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:13.870
Secondly, we have then struggled, I think,
to develop simple frameworks which are capable

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:15.430
for wider spread.

00:08:15.430 --> 00:08:22.509
If I could give you a little example: we have
at least two of our vanguard systems that

00:08:22.509 --> 00:08:28.180
are at the moment engaging different people
to try and develop new organisational forms,

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:32.760
and they are trying to solve a very similar
problem.

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:37.070
What we are looking to do through the way
the programme is constructed is to bring people

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:45.120
together with national support so that we
can develop single solutions where possible,

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:51.740
which are generic archetypes which can be
then tailored to local use, and for those

00:08:51.740 --> 00:08:59.290
to be made available not just to the vanguards
themselves but to the wider NHS, along with

00:08:59.290 --> 00:09:02.209
the stories of how they are then being implemented.

00:09:02.209 --> 00:09:08.550
So the communication of what the vanguards
are doing, and the products of the support

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:13.450
programme, will be absolutely critical to
the success of the programme.

00:09:13.450 --> 00:09:22.959
I spent an evening with six CCGs who were
getting together to do some knowledge-sharing,

00:09:22.959 --> 00:09:24.540
actually.

00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:27.959
Some were vanguards, some were aspiring, some
were just interested and wanted to watch,

00:09:27.959 --> 00:09:31.399
and there were a few common themes that came
out.

00:09:31.399 --> 00:09:35.680
One was that support must be wanted and helpful,
as opposed to, ‘We are from head office

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:37.230
and we are here to support you.’

00:09:37.230 --> 00:09:44.290
There was a little bit of a message around
making sure we drop some maybe traditional

00:09:44.290 --> 00:09:49.019
behaviour; apparently the guidance for the
latest round changed three days before the

00:09:49.019 --> 00:09:53.990
deadline for getting the submissions in, which
puts pressure on people.

00:09:53.990 --> 00:09:55.490
They were the minor points.

00:09:55.490 --> 00:10:03.339
The major point was how good the support package
was and how the co creation was working, but

00:10:03.339 --> 00:10:06.620
there were two things that came across consistently.

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:12.380
One was the need for support for people who
were managing complicated multi-professional

00:10:12.380 --> 00:10:19.600
teams for the very first time, and that was
a common theme which we can anticipate everybody

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:24.209
else will possibly want as well.

00:10:24.209 --> 00:10:29.300
Linked to that was what was described as ‘amazement’
at the cultural differences between different

00:10:29.300 --> 00:10:33.880
geographies and different clinical tribes,
and so on.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:41.190
The second one was workforce issues, which
plays into Health Education England, Royal

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:46.029
Colleges, professional bodies, and again,
saying, ‘Actually, we can see where we could

00:10:46.029 --> 00:10:50.430
bring this, but we will need permission, we
will need people getting out of the way, we

00:10:50.430 --> 00:10:52.600
will need a lot of things to be different.’

00:10:52.600 --> 00:10:57.269
It plays into this thing about, can we get
those changes in place so that we do not find

00:10:57.269 --> 00:11:02.190
we are suddenly putting brakes on vanguards
in six months’ time, or regulations are

00:11:02.190 --> 00:11:06.190
putting brakes on vanguards as well?

00:11:06.190 --> 00:11:11.339
I suppose it is a plea to try and anticipate
what some of those interventions may be, and

00:11:11.339 --> 00:11:16.920
then when people ask for them, to have them
ready ahead of time rather than having to

00:11:16.920 --> 00:11:18.620
start once somebody asks.

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:23.970
Absolutely, and there is a fine balance here,
Ciarán, between the extent to which the national

00:11:23.970 --> 00:11:28.450
bodies can be aware and mindful of some of
the issues that are going to come up, but

00:11:28.450 --> 00:11:33.240
at the same time not impose them as their
preconceptions of what the vanguards might

00:11:33.240 --> 00:11:34.400
themselves want.

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:39.589
I think the answer in part is the method of
the joint working between national and local;

00:11:39.589 --> 00:11:44.839
so if we get the right people in the room,
the right national people – whose rules

00:11:44.839 --> 00:11:52.860
and processes, systems and mindsets are often
the things that are causing difficulty for

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:57.680
local systems and the things that they want
changed – if we get those people in the

00:11:57.680 --> 00:12:03.160
room with the local systems, to be able to
talk through what the barriers are and what

00:12:03.160 --> 00:12:07.810
the opportunities are, to try and fix those
issues, I think we have a strong chance of

00:12:07.810 --> 00:12:08.810
success.

00:12:08.810 --> 00:12:14.089
That is exactly what we are looking to do
on the workforce issues that you describe.

00:12:14.089 --> 00:12:16.870
Just a minor point, really.

00:12:16.870 --> 00:12:22.329
I think this is really good; I think it is
a really good description of intention and

00:12:22.329 --> 00:12:25.360
process matching it.

00:12:25.360 --> 00:12:32.709
In my view, these things go wrong and we do
not talk about the difficulties and the challenges,

00:12:32.709 --> 00:12:37.290
so what I am interesting in knowing is how
and when, or what is the intention behind

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:41.829
bringing some of the challenges in the system
not necessarily to a public meeting, but to

00:12:41.829 --> 00:12:45.139
NHS England’s attention so we can actually
address them?

00:12:45.139 --> 00:12:50.860
Because I think it is usually underestimated
the challenge to our own culture, as the Chair

00:12:50.860 --> 00:12:51.860
has pointed out.

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:56.319
These things tend to start brilliantly and
then get tougher and tougher, because actually

00:12:56.319 --> 00:12:59.500
the system leadership really does not like
what it is seeing.

00:12:59.500 --> 00:13:05.019
So I am interested in how we are going to
get the learning back as to what is difficult

00:13:05.019 --> 00:13:10.980
and challenging, and in particular, the point
that you made in your paper which I think

00:13:10.980 --> 00:13:16.379
is, again, really welcome, which is how we
are engaging the communities and the systems

00:13:16.379 --> 00:13:17.980
outside the NHS.

00:13:17.980 --> 00:13:24.260
It is a wonderfully well stated intention;
I am really interested in the experience of

00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:29.649
it and what we are learning really about how
that is working and what the methodologies

00:13:29.649 --> 00:13:35.310
that we are applying actually are.

00:13:35.310 --> 00:13:43.190
On your second point, Victor, around engaging
people and communities: there are a wide range

00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:48.829
of different views as to the best methods
and approaches for tackling that.

00:13:48.829 --> 00:13:55.230
We are absolutely engaging the People and
Communities Board that was set up as part

00:13:55.230 --> 00:14:01.010
of the forward view governance to try and
help provide support, to make sure that we

00:14:01.010 --> 00:14:04.569
have articulated the full range of different
approaches people can take.

00:14:04.569 --> 00:14:10.519
We talk about the directory of support that
is going to be available.

00:14:10.519 --> 00:14:16.560
We need then through the measurement and evaluation
processes, as well as the informal feedback

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:22.130
loops, to be able to understand how well that
is really operating and working.

00:14:22.130 --> 00:14:33.329
There is, I think, a risk that as we create
more integrated forms of provision that can

00:14:33.329 --> 00:14:39.000
help with better co ordination of care, that
some of those integrated forms of provision

00:14:39.000 --> 00:14:45.250
may not necessarily represent the full wishes
and preferences of their local communities,

00:14:45.250 --> 00:14:51.970
and it becomes vitally important that the
new systems that are being developed fully

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:59.820
take into account the importance of transferring
power to patients and to local communities.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:05.509
That will be an integral part of the programme
and we will be looking to embed some metrics

00:15:05.509 --> 00:15:09.629
in relation to that as part of the evaluation
piece.

00:15:09.629 --> 00:15:15.810
I think that, through the work of the different
workstreams and the continued engagement of

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:20.759
this Board, including through the Commissioning
Committee and the regular conversations in

00:15:20.759 --> 00:15:26.949
the Commissioning Committee, the Board will
be fully sighted on what some of the roadblocks

00:15:26.949 --> 00:15:33.540
that we are experiencing are, and will be
able to make judgements about the rate of

00:15:33.540 --> 00:15:38.490
progress, as well as understanding where we
are needing to escalate issues.

00:15:38.490 --> 00:15:44.670
If I could give one example – and I mentioned
this yesterday in our development session,

00:15:44.670 --> 00:15:50.449
Chair – the way in which the NHS currently
manages transactions can feel very slow.

00:15:50.449 --> 00:15:56.629
There are many reasons why the proposals that
Torbay have had to develop and integrated

00:15:56.629 --> 00:16:02.819
care organisation have taken I think over
three years and are still not yet approved,

00:16:02.819 --> 00:16:06.529
each of which makes sense in their own right.

00:16:06.529 --> 00:16:13.949
However, if this programme is going to work
at the pace at which we require it to, we

00:16:13.949 --> 00:16:20.129
will have to fundamentally redesign some of
those processes, and that poses a major challenge

00:16:20.129 --> 00:16:26.319
to ourselves, to the Department of Health,
and indeed to Monitor and the Trust Development

00:16:26.319 --> 00:16:27.319
Authority.

00:16:27.319 --> 00:16:28.319
Thank you.

00:16:28.319 --> 00:16:34.560
What we are trying to do with the vanguards
is to defragment the system with the vanguards

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:41.980
as a wedge, I think, for a viral impact across
the rest of the highly fragmented model of

00:16:41.980 --> 00:16:42.980
the NHS.

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:48.990
I think just in summary, Ian, the Board sees
that the vanguard programme is absolutely

00:16:48.990 --> 00:16:53.939
central and absolutely critical to the mission
that we are engaged in.

00:16:53.939 --> 00:16:56.829
We see it as potentially completely transformational.

00:16:56.829 --> 00:17:02.600
We emphasise very strongly the replication
question that was raised by Noel, and the

00:17:02.600 --> 00:17:08.780
dissemination of knowledge and the diffusion
and the viral effect that we hope will come

00:17:08.780 --> 00:17:09.780
from that.

00:17:09.780 --> 00:17:15.790
I look forward to the announcement of the
Urgent and Emergency Care vanguards and future

00:17:15.790 --> 00:17:22.800
variants on the model, because it is a very
different way of doing things, to which our

00:17:22.800 --> 00:17:26.100
traditional structures and silos are not well
adapted.

00:17:26.100 --> 00:17:33.520
I think we are going to want to give you every
support, but also watch very closely, and

00:17:33.520 --> 00:17:39.290
in particular take care of the relationship
between the vanguards and ourselves and our

00:17:39.290 --> 00:17:45.880
need to be flexible, fluid and fleet of foot
in responding to their expectations of us,

00:17:45.880 --> 00:17:49.590
having made the promises to them that we have.

00:17:49.590 --> 00:17:57.010
I am conscious that you are fully cognisant
of that, and thanks so much to your team for

00:17:57.010 --> 00:18:01.960
all the work they have done in bringing us
to this position.

00:18:01.960 --> 00:18:08.550
I think you can reassure them that our focus
on them and expectations will not diminish.

00:18:08.550 --> 00:18:11.030
Thank you very much for that.

